<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/thompson1917-ch04-04--grok> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grok 4.7 on thompson1917-ch04-04: not-empirically-resolvable, resolvability 0.22";
    dc:description "Morphological axes are easy to score, but the claim does not add a measurement that would be true of a force field and false of other polarity mechanisms. Particle chains are both the phenomenon and the supposed proof. The statement therefore has no discriminating empirical consequence.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: The claim identifies visible cell polarity with a polarised force distribution whose lines are the aligned particles. Molecular polarity, cytoskeletal tracks and a physical force field all satisfy that description, so no outcome separates the claim from its rivals.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-grok-4.7-high-fast>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "Aligned organelles are the observation the wording treats as the evidence of the forces.", "Any successful polarity mechanism can be redescribed as dynamical polarity.";
